  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "pageVersion": "sha256:99a09c28bef1a338f08b3a85d7d12199c5edaf2e5640eb7132a338d8bf62c602",
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_cd5033fcda30403a9af2fa736488124a",
      "statement": "Coherent measures built on the marginal return distribution summarize risk at a single observation instant and ignore the temporal order of losses, whereas the managed-account manager's relevant risk depends on the magnitude and duration of the account's drawdown, since a client's decision to close the account likely depends on these.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L16-L16",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:de9f6590e9becd7380d7fb8b42e81e30492eb19856235a9d7100a3aec41a0178:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_cddc90c4a5ff4820ab651f7aef87091b",
      "statement": "Operational thresholds make the concern concrete: a Commodity Trading Advisor rarely keeps a client whose account has been in drawdown for over two years, a client is unlikely to tolerate a 50% drawdown at a medium- or low-risk CTA, and a proprietary-system trader can be suspended when a maximum-drawdown condition (typically around 20% of collateral capital) is breached, with a warning at a lower level around 15%.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L18-L18",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f4cd65e801289a34fef1f862ec99d707c9325331b18b76822c782721abedec3c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a54873134aa04603bfb37f7b9df1e24c",
      "statement": "Unlike Grossman-Zhou and Cvitanic-Karatzas, who seek time-dependent allocation strategies under return-distribution assumptions, this approach defines risk and finds constant-in-time optimal weights on a single sample path of portfolio returns with no assumption on the underlying probability distribution, so risk is a function of the sample path rather than a measure over a set of paths.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L20-L20",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5ae1b66e655bacd5d72fca7aa4ab208c54d4020887c3fdfe6c950a14e08bdd0c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ec684aecbaa742e5a0443244ccc8dd32",
      "statement": "The drawdown function D(x,t) = max_{0≤τ≤t}{w(x,τ)} − w(x,t) is the fall of the portfolio value relative to its past maximum; it is non-negative and vanishes exactly when the portfolio hits a new maximum.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L24-L27",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6ae6503e8ebcf38ee5f1584815df1081fbf94ed6788228d9a1ea03d49a2a806e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_04515bd0c5394975a1d234bef131ba7b",
      "statement": "The drawdown is path-dependent because the running maximum max_{0≤τ≤t}{w(x,τ)} depends on the entire trajectory preceding t, and cannot be reconstructed from the marginal return distribution alone.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L27-L27",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6fc4ba97c2270f2ab4934cc98c09a84b20cf24845ca9b9009ef4755d6df967ad:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_892c75b9351841bd8a0e25f57bb0b9a7",
      "statement": "Three risk functions are built on a sample path — the maximum drawdown M(x)=max_{0≤t≤T}{D(x,t)}, the average drawdown A(x)=(1/T)∫_0^T D(x,t)dt, and the Conditional Drawdown-at-Risk, a family parametrized by α containing the other two as limit cases.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L29-L40",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:183639d5ba0760693764c4b08fb93dc474731673f336e47776924060d4532ff6:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d3d2d6f91c8a40cc8a2981e70e347f60",
      "statement": "The α-CDaR is the average of the worst (1−α)·100% drawdowns experienced on the path (e.g. the 95%-CDaR is the average of the worst 5% of drawdowns).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L46-L46",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b73bd9d2639ce551ede95235a998e9ef5c8dc9214a048435016b09263ccc1999:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4bad4de3ddbe44069b2200b316e6b4b0",
      "statement": "In the general non-integer case the CDaR takes the optimization form Δ_α(x) = min_ζ {ζ + (1/((1−α)T))∫_0^T [D(x,t)−ζ]⁺ dt}, where the optimal ζ equals ζ_α(x) when (1−α)N is integer and is otherwise ≥ ζ_α(x).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L51-L53",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:733da6d551de84ba15ab89987cf2b3e6cfd9d5b17ae3e2c67537f60fe62d256f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3023dc139e23417982dd5dca8d141a5b",
      "statement": "The CDaR family interpolates between the elementary measures: as α→1 the CDaR tends to the maximum drawdown Δ_1(x)=M(x), and at α=0 it coincides with the average drawdown Δ_0(x)=A(x).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L55-L55",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:93cb548ed608ae64b9cbf6110ff57532049efafb19987955642d2b5f0b7efff2:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_79bd5598a4b74ae38501e4fa3daaa04c",
      "statement": "Because the uncompounded cumulative portfolio return w(x,t)=y(t)·x is linear in the weights, the running maximum is a pointwise max of linear functions and hence convex, so the drawdown function D(x,t) and the measures MaxDD, AvDD and CDaR are all convex in x, making the return-CDaR problem a convex optimization with linear objective and piecewise-linear convex constraint.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L59-L62",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:529e2a9e858f338677fad07120d6efc31feed74b5032705fc570e50a747eec6e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2202c9309fd64d06825e0ba2a1c40162",
      "statement": "Linearity of w(x,t) in x gives positive homogeneity: w(λx,t)=λw(x,t) implies D(λx,t)=λD(x,t), so M, A and Δ_α are positively homogeneous of degree one in the weights, and the CDaR is akin to the CVaR (also known as Mean Excess Loss/Mean Shortfall and Tail Value-at-Risk for continuous distributions).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L63-L64",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a417ba7c33647e728c5f3c38d3873a60cd39824641d71eb00a333959fccf47bf:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_dc3ce8cc0af840329098edc1e0e27930",
      "statement": "The maximum-drawdown-constrained problem reduces to a linear program (16) with auxiliary variables u_k where the constraints u_k ≥ y_k·x and monotonicity u_k ≥ u_{k-1} force u_k to the running maximum, so that u_k − y_k·x reconstructs the discrete drawdown D_k(x)=max_{1≤j≤k}{y_j·x}−y_k·x.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L68-L86",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:43cd9f9fd9024c946cb0d3f54ed11033bb0ae224c115d5ac5f139528767d4a55:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_87a48b0d12c9404b9fc4fdbf4013e523",
      "statement": "The CDaR-constrained problem reduces to the linear program (18) where variables z_k ≥ u_k−y_k·x−ζ, z_k ≥ 0 linearize the positive part [D_k(x)−ζ]⁺ of the CDaR, and the formulation does not involve the threshold function ζ_α(x): at the optimum x and ζ give an optimal portfolio and the corresponding threshold value.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L95-L107",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a785f9ad9611b5836600f4fdc35b3435a4325b696deee5a4c955b1e596776bb6:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_8e3c7dcf6fa949c9906873efa8793b26",
      "statement": "The constants ν_1, ν_2, ν_3 ∈ [0,1] define the portions of capital one is willing to lose, multiple constraints can be combined, and the reduction to a linear program allows solving problems with many thousands of instruments.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L107-L107",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e163c9b9ba81a824a1c0559519c4a1082d1dbed674871b0e46f763553af62663:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_188ac634a4904898b4583a024c269f2e",
      "statement": "The three measures give constrained formulations maximizing return subject to M(x)≤ν_1 C, A(x)≤ν_2 C or Δ_α(x)≤ν_3 C, and dually serve as objectives through reward/risk ratios MaxDDRatio=R(x)/M(x), AvDDRatio=R(x)/A(x), CDaRRatio=R(x)/Δ_α(x), the maximum-ratio portfolio being the tangency of the line through the origin with the efficient frontier.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L111-L113",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:775e86cb30844f765e84a8f74812df779e0fca560233dbe8f4034b18a2b01421:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_363a0603469d4ed69b1800651f7aa53c",
      "statement": "In the numerical study on futures across 32 markets (1/1/1988–1/9/1999) with x_min=0.2 and x_max=0.8, the LPs (16), (17), (18) were solved with CPLEX and independently verified via a genetic algorithm yielding identical weights, with the box constraints bounding leverage and making the efficient frontier concave.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L115-L115",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:491d0225229d21110015a0f7552fcee62e95cc697e498080a73c5de69d39dead:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1563c1e412bf4535968da6383497bfc6",
      "statement": "The 5% CDaR constraint ((1−α)=0.05) yields weights significantly different from MaxDD, involving dozens of events in the average and producing a portfolio more robust than both MaxDD and AvDD; because MaxDD rests on a single observation of the maximum loss its solutions can carry substantial statistical error, while the CDaR family's statistical averaging gives better future-risk prediction and more stable weights at an appropriate confidence level such as α=0.95.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/20_drawdown_risk_measures.md#L117-L117",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:215195d3cf24cc3c3c0729e91b7d1673455efeec4741bb6fb199a949896b1b47:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
